Stock vendor VINTF manifest fragments. These are NOT copied to the device as
fragments — device.mk lists them in DEVICE_MANIFEST_FILE, and assemble_vintf
merges all of them plus ../manifest.xml into one /vendor/etc/vintf/manifest.xml.

Two mechanisms are unavailable:
  - the vendor tree, because extract-utils force-modules etc/vintf/manifest/*
    and 11 of the module names collide with the HAL libraries themselves
  - PRODUCT_COPY_FILES, because build/make/core/Makefile:72 rejects VINTF
    metadata copied that way

31 of stock's 33 are here. Two are deliberately absent, both because the HAL is
built from SOURCE and the source module installs its own fragment into
/vendor/etc/vintf/manifest/:

    android.hardware.health@2.1.xml        (PRODUCT_PACKAGES health@2.1-impl/-service)
    android.hardware.cas@1.2-service.xml   (platform cas HAL)

Declaring either one here as well is not harmless duplication -- VINTF unions the
merged manifest.xml with the fragment directory at runtime and rejects the
overlap outright:

    Cannot add manifest fragment /vendor/etc/vintf/manifest/android.hardware.cas@1.2-service.xml:
    HAL "android.hardware.cas" has a conflict: Conflicting major version:
    1.2 (from /vendor/etc/vintf/manifest.xml) vs. 1.2 (from .../android.hardware.cas@1.2-service.xml)

which fails check_vintf during target-files packaging with the far less obvious
"No device manifest file from device or from update package". Both dropped files
are byte-identical to what the source module installs.

Before adding a fragment back, check what the build actually installs:
    ls $OUT/vendor/etc/vintf/manifest/
